<p>At Byrra, the place of execution at Myra, a crowd had gathered to watch three men die. The condemned stood with linen cloths over their faces. The executioner had already raised his sword when the bishop of Myra reached the place, running. He took the sword out of the executioner’s hands, threw it to the ground, and set the three men free.</p>

<p>That is the oldest story the world has of Nicholas of Myra. It comes from a Greek text known as the <em>Praxis de stratelatis</em>, the “Story of the Generals.” An unknown author wrote it sometime between the late fourth century and the late sixth, and a writer in Constantinople was quoting it after 583. There is no Santa Claus in it, and no bag of gold. There is a bishop who runs toward a sword.</p>

<p>Today Nicholas is one of the most loved saints on earth. The Orthodox Church honors him above almost every saint except the Mother of God and John the Baptist, and calls him simply the Wonderworker. In the West he is Saint Nicholas of Bari, patron of sailors and merchants, bakers and travelers and children, and of lands from Greece and Russia to Sicily and Lorraine. Yet when the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em> came to write about him in 1911, it admitted how little lies under all that love.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“…scarcely anything historically certain about him except that he was Bishop of Myra in the fourth century.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Michael Ott, <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, 1911</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>How does a man of whom almost nothing is certain become the saint that half of Christendom calls on in trouble? The stories of Nicholas came in layers, over a thousand years: an early account of a rescue, a ninth-century Life, medieval legends, and a bold voyage to carry off his bones. Some layers are old and sober, and some are late and fanciful. What is striking is that all of them show the same man. He runs toward people in danger. He gives in the dark and asks for silence. He calls himself a sinner. And when people call on him, he comes. To find him, we have to walk through the layers, beginning with the town where the oldest Life says he was born.</p>

<h2 id="an-only-son-in-patara">An Only Son in Patara</h2>

<p>The first full Life of Nicholas was written in the early ninth century by a monk named Michael the Archimandrite, nearly five hundred years after Nicholas died. Michael tells us that Nicholas was born in Patara, a town of Lycia, the only son of Christian parents who were rich. Tradition puts his birth about the year 270. Michael gives no names for the parents. The Russian tradition, which comes from Dimitri of Rostov in the seventeenth century, calls them Theophanes and Nonna, and the Russian Church keeps a feast of his birth on 29 July.</p>

<p>Michael adds one story about the baby. On Wednesdays and Fridays, the weekly fast days, the infant Nicholas refused his mother’s breast, or took it only once. Monks loved to tell such stories about the saints they admired, and the Bari scholar Gerardo Cioffari calls this one a monastic commonplace. It tells us more about the monks who prayed to Nicholas than about a child in Patara. But it tells us how they saw him: as a man who belonged to God from his first days.</p>

<p>Then his parents died. The only son was left alone, and he was left rich. What a man of faith should do with a fortune is a question as old as the Gospel, and the first story that Michael tells of the grown Nicholas is his answer.</p>

<h2 id="gold-through-a-window-at-night">Gold Through a Window at Night</h2>

<p>In his city there lived a man with three daughters and no dowry to give any of them. Nicholas came to the house at night, threw a bag of gold through the window, and was gone before anyone saw him.</p>

<p>He came back, in the same way, with a second bag. When the third night came, the father stayed awake. He heard the gold land in the house. The sound gave Nicholas away, and the father caught him and fell at his feet.</p>

<p>Nicholas lifted him up. All he asked was silence: he made the father swear that he would tell no one what had happened. The father, as Michael tells it, called him the savior of himself and his three daughters, after God. That small phrase matters. In the story the gift is God’s, and Nicholas is only the hand that threw it through the window.</p>

<p>The oath of silence is the one wish of Nicholas’s that the world has refused to grant. The story of the gold has been told across Christendom ever since, and it explains why Nicholas became the saint of gift-giving, and, at some remove, the distant source of Santa Claus. So the man who wanted no one to know became the most famous giver in the world. Yet the next story Michael tells is a public one. The Church of Myra came looking for him.</p>

<h2 id="the-first-man-through-the-door">The First Man Through the Door</h2>

<p>The bishop of Myra died, and the bishops gathered to choose his successor. One of them had a dream. He was told to stand at the door of the church during the night, and that the first man to come through it would be named Nicholas. That man was to be their bishop.</p>

<p>The bishops spent the whole night in prayer. At dawn a young man came to the church, the first to arrive, and the bishop who had dreamed the dream asked him who he was. Michael gives his answer.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Nicholas, a sinner, a servant of your Holiness, O master.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Nicholas at the church door, in the <em>Life</em> by Michael the Archimandrite, early ninth century</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The young man who had thrown gold through a window to stay unseen was taken into the church and made bishop of the chief city of Lycia. Michael told it as a story of God’s choosing, and the frame of a dream and a door is his. But the core of it has other support. Gratian’s <em>Decretum</em>, a medieval book of Church law, remembers Nicholas as a layman made bishop by the acclamation of the people, and Cioffari thinks that part is probably true.</p>

<p>Notice the word he chose for himself. “A sinner” is how Nicholas names himself here, at the moment of his election. He will use the same words again, much later, in a stranger setting: in the dreams of an emperor.</p>

<h2 id="a-bishop-in-a-city-of-artemis">A Bishop in a City of Artemis</h2>

<p>Myra, today the town of Demre in Turkey, was an ancient city, and its great temple belonged to Artemis. Michael describes it as the tallest of temples, and ancient inscriptions from Myra bear out its size and fame. According to Michael, Nicholas tore it down, and the demons that had lived in it fled. Michael also remembers him as a bishop who fought the teachings of Arius and Sabellius, two teachers whose ideas about the Trinity the Church rejected.</p>

<p>Michael had heard one more thing, from a monk who passed on what was still remembered of Nicholas. People said the bishop was venerable to look at, that a sweet fragrance hung about him, and that heretics who met him went away healed of their sickness of unbelief. It is a hearsay portrait, written down centuries later. It is also the closest thing we have to a face.</p>

<p>Later Greek writers added a time of suffering. In their accounts Nicholas was imprisoned in the persecution under the emperor Diocletian and confessed his faith there. The story first appears around the year 900, and Alban Butler, in his <em>Lives of the Saints</em>, was honest about it.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“The silence of other authors make many justly suspect these circumstances.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Alban Butler, <em>The Lives of the Saints</em>, 6 December</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The same care applies to the best-known scene in the later Lives. For many readers it has made Nicholas a hero of the faith: his place among the bishops at the Council of Nicaea.</p>

<h2 id="the-council-he-may-have-attended">The Council He May Have Attended</h2>

<p>In 325 the bishops met in council at Nicaea over the teaching of Arius. Michael, who wrote the first full Life and knew that Nicholas had opposed Arius, never mentions the council at all. The first writer known to put Nicholas at Nicaea is Nicetas of Paphlagonia, before 890. The Synaxarion, the Church’s book of short lives for each day, repeats it around 900. By the late tenth century Symeon Metaphrastes could describe him as a member of the council who helped bring its work about.</p>

<p>Nor do the lists of bishops at Nicaea settle the question. The oldest and shortest lists, with about two hundred names, leave him out. As the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em> noted, “his name is not mentioned in any of the old lists of bishops that attended this council.” Longer lists, with about three hundred names, do include him. So does the list of Theodore the Lector, written about 515, the earliest source to name him at the council. The scholars disagree about that entry. Anrich, who edited the Greek texts about Nicholas, thought another hand added it later. Cioffari thinks it is genuine, and guesses that Theodore found it in the ancient Life that a writer in Constantinople quoted after 583.</p>

<p>So Nicholas may well have sat among the bishops at Nicaea. The Church’s memory has long placed him there, and it may be right, but history cannot confirm it. One well-loved story says that he struck Arius at the council, was jailed for it, and was restored when Christ and the Mother of God appeared to him with a Gospel book and a bishop’s stole. It is a late legend, first written down perhaps in the fourteenth century.</p>

<p>But the oldest story of Nicholas has nothing to do with councils. It is about three men, a governor, and a sword.</p>

<h2 id="silver-and-gold-at-byrra">Silver and Gold at Byrra</h2>

<p>The governor in the story is a man named Eustathius, and he had condemned three innocent men of Myra to death. Three imperial officers, Nepotianus, Ursus, and Eupoleonis, were in Myra at the time. When word came to Nicholas, he ran. The <em>Praxis</em> shows him running, and he reached Byrra just as the sword was raised over men whose faces were already covered for death. He took the sword, threw it down, and freed them.</p>

<p>Then he went to the governor. He pushed open the door of the praetorium, the governor’s hall, and when Eustathius came forward to honor him, Nicholas refused it. The governor named two men, Eudoxius and Simonides, as the ones responsible. Nicholas would not let him hide behind them. Those two had not done this, he said. Silver and gold had. The governor had been bought, and the bishop said so to his face and threatened to report him to the emperor.</p>

<p>Eustathius gave way and repented. The three officers asked Nicholas to forgive him, and he did. The fierce bishop who had thrown the executioner’s sword on the ground pardoned the man who had ordered the killing. Cioffari thought that this hot temper was true to everything else remembered of Nicholas.</p>

<p>The three officers went on their way. They could not know that they would soon stand where the three men of Byrra had stood.</p>

<h2 id="a-stranger-in-the-emperors-dream">A Stranger in the Emperor’s Dream</h2>

<p>In Constantinople, Nepotianus, Ursus, and Eupoleonis were framed. False charges were brought against them, and they were condemned to die. They remembered the bishop of Myra, and they prayed to the Lord God of Saint Nicholas to hear them.</p>

<p>That night the emperor Constantine saw a stranger in a dream. He demanded to know how this man had got into his palace. The visitor gave his name as Bishop Nicholas, a sinner, who lived in the chief city of Lycia, and he had come on behalf of the three condemned men. The same night he came in a dream to the prefect Ablabius and named himself again: Nicholas, a sinner, a servant of God, the bishop of Myra. Then he was gone.</p>

<p>In the morning Constantine had the three officers brought to him, and he wanted to know what magic they were using against him. In an early sermon in honor of Nicholas, attributed to Saint Proclus of Constantinople and perhaps preached around 440, the emperor’s question is short and baffled.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“How is it that you have sent a certain Nicholas to punish me?”</p>
  <p><cite>— Emperor Constantine to the three officers, in the <em>Encomium on Saint Nicholas</em> attributed to Proclus of Constantinople</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Constantine set them free, and he told them plainly where their lives had come from: from the man they had called on, Saint Nicholas, and not from the emperor.</p>

<p>Money runs through the whole story. At Byrra it bought a governor, and the <em>Praxis</em> even records the size of a bribe, two hundred pounds of silver and seventeen hundred pounds of gold, a detail that the popular retellings leave out. The story also has firm ground under it. Nepotianus and Ablabius were real men, known to the historians Zosimus and Eunapius. Cioffari accepts the frame of the story as historical, though he reads the dream visits as the storyteller’s way of showing a saint’s power. Of all the stories about Nicholas, the rescue at Byrra is the one the Roman Martyrology chooses to tell on his feast.</p>

<p>Twice in one night the bishop of Myra stood before the most powerful men in the empire, and twice he called himself what he had called himself at the church door: a sinner. Later stories widened the circle of his care, from three condemned men to a whole hungry city.</p>

<h2 id="grain-ships-at-andriake">Grain Ships at Andriake</h2>

<p>Famine came to Myra. At Andriake, the harbor of Myra, grain ships from Alexandria lay at anchor, carrying their cargo to Constantinople. Nicholas went to them with a request: let each ship unload a hundred measures of grain for his hungry city. He promised that they would suffer no loss for it.</p>

<p>They unloaded the grain. When the ships reached Constantinople, Michael says, their cargo was found to be the same as when it had left Alexandria. And the grain left at Andriake fed Myra for two years.</p>

<p>The story was probably being told by the sixth century. In the early eighth, Andrew of Crete told it with a touch of his own. In his version Nicholas stood among the sailors in the manner of a sailor, and left three gold coins on the ship. The bishop who gave in secret gave here too, quietly, with his own hands. And his care was turning more and more toward the harbor, and toward the men who went to sea.</p>

<h2 id="the-sailors-who-called-his-name">The Sailors Who Called His Name</h2>

<p>Michael tells of a ship caught in a storm. The sailors called out to Nicholas, the bishop of Myra, to save them. Then he was there among them, and he spoke.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Behold, you have called me, and I am here to help you.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Nicholas to the sailors in the storm, in the <em>Life</em> by Michael the Archimandrite</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The sailors were saved. Later they came to the church at Myra and found Nicholas there. He was dressed so plainly that he looked like one of the many clergy, and at first they did not know which of them was the bishop. Then they recognized him as the man who had appeared to them and helped them in the storm.</p>

<p>He did not let them leave with only a miracle. He told them, Michael says, that their greed and their lust were the kind of sins that invite God’s correction. The rescuer was also a pastor, and he scolded the men he had saved. It is one of the most human moments in all the stories about him.</p>

<p>This story, more than any other, made Nicholas the saint of sailors. Myra was a stopover for pilgrims bound for the Holy Land, and the travelers who passed through it took him as their own. Generations of seamen would call his name in storms, and he would be remembered as the saint who comes when called. But first the bishop himself had to die.</p>

<h2 id="angels-at-the-last-hour">Angels at the Last Hour</h2>

<p>Michael says almost nothing about the end. Nicholas left his mortal life, and his body was buried in his church, where it gave off a sweet perfume. Symeon Metaphrastes, in the tenth century, adds a short illness, and has Nicholas die singing thanksgivings to God, carried to his grave by bishops and clergy with torches. He died on 6 December. The year is not known.</p>

<p>The <em>Golden Legend</em>, the collection of saints’ lives compiled by Jacobus de Voragine in 1275, gives the scene that medieval Europe remembered.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“…he prayed our Lord that he would send him his angels; and inclining his head he saw the angels come to him, whereby he knew well that he should depart.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Jacobus de Voragine, <em>The Golden Legend</em>, 1275</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>He began the psalm <em>In te, Domine, speravi</em>, “In you, O Lord, I have hoped,” and prayed it as far as the words <em>in manus tuas</em>, “into your hands.” Then he died. What happened next, in this telling, is the beginning of a story that has never stopped.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“…a fountain of oil sprang out from the head unto his feet; and unto this day holy oil issueth out of his body…”</p>
  <p><cite>— Jacobus de Voragine, <em>The Golden Legend</em>, 1275</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The fragrant liquid from his tomb drew pilgrims to Myra for seven centuries. Then, in the spring of 1087, it drew armed men from Italy.</p>

<h2 id="pilgrims-at-the-tomb-in-myra">Pilgrims at the Tomb in Myra</h2>

<p>Long before then, the bishop of Myra was honored far beyond Lycia. His story was told in sermons, and an emperor built a church in his honor in Constantinople, by one account Justinian in the sixth century. By the seventh or eighth century his face was painted on an icon at Sinai, and in the eighth his image appeared in a fresco in the church of Santa Maria Antiqua in Rome. Early in the eighth century Andrew of Crete preached in his honor at the tomb itself, and praised him as one who had turned hurricanes into breezes.</p>

<p>As his fame grew, his story grew with it. In the sixth century there lived another Nicholas, the abbot of Holy Sion, who made a pilgrimage to Jerusalem and was remembered for calming a storm at sea and for many healings. Later writers merged the two lives into one, and by the tenth century the bishop of Myra had been given the abbot’s pilgrimage and the abbot’s miracles. In 1751 a scholar named Falconius even argued that the two were one man, and others, among them Assemani and Putignani, answered him. Here the abbot’s stories are left to the abbot.</p>

<p>For centuries the tomb at Myra was one of the great shrines of the Christian East. Then the world around it changed. In 1036, by one account, Lycia was overrun by Saracens. The monks who kept the tomb came to fear for their lives, and the cities of the West began to look at the shrine with a different kind of interest.</p>

<h2 id="forty-seven-men-from-bari">Forty-Seven Men from Bari</h2>

<p>In the spring of 1087 a fleet of merchant ships from the Italian city of Bari was sailing for Antioch with grain and other goods. The men of Bari had a plan that went beyond trade: they meant to take the bones of Saint Nicholas home with them. They had heard that the Venetians intended the same thing and had even brought iron tools for the work. The Bari ships got there first.</p>

<p>In April forty-seven armed men went up from the ships to the church. Most of the monks had withdrawn into the mountains for fear of raiders. Only four remained to guard the tomb.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“…forty-seven knights of Bari, and four monks showed to them the sepulchre of S. Nicholas.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Jacobus de Voragine, <em>The Golden Legend</em>, 1275</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Showing was not quite how it went. A Bari man named Matthew drew his sword on one of the monks and demanded the tomb. The monk told them of a vision seen a year before. Then a priest of Bari named Lupus dropped a glass vial of the saint’s oil on the stone floor, and it did not break. The men of Bari took it as a sign that Nicholas consented to go with them.</p>

<p>Matthew took a hammer and smashed the lid of the tomb. Inside, the coffin was full of liquid up to the navel of the body, and a fragrance rose from it so strong that it reached the men waiting at the ships, three miles away. So wrote Nicephorus of Bari, who set the story down that same year. The sailors carried the bones down to the ships and left the monks of Myra to their grief.</p>

<h2 id="twenty-days-to-bari">Twenty Days to Bari</h2>

<p>The voyage home did not go smoothly. At a place called Perdikca the ship carrying the relics lay stuck in a calm. At last five sailors confessed that they had taken fragments of the relics for themselves. They gave them back and swore on the Gospels that they had kept nothing, and the fleet went on. One of the sailors, a man named Disigius, dreamed that in twenty days they would all arrive at Bari together.</p>

<p>They came into Bari on 9 May 1087. Crowds came down to meet them, and a quarrel broke out at once with the archbishop of Bari, Ursus. The relics were carried through the harbor gate to the praetorium, where the altar of Saint Eustratius was torn down to make room for his. For a time the abbot Elias of the monastery of Saint Benedict kept them in his care.</p>

<p>That first night, Nicephorus and a Greek account agree, forty-seven people were cured. One of them, Nicephorus writes, was a young man who had been possessed for five years, deaf, mute, and blind, and who was healed on the Thursday. A great basilica rose over the bones, and Pope Urban II laid them beneath its main altar. Among those who saw the translation was Theodore, an envoy of Metropolitan John II of Kiev, whose account of it survives in Russian.</p>

<p>The sailors of Bari were given a privilege: the right to be buried along the walls of the basilica. In one Bari family, the Poveromo, family tradition keeps a bone carved with the saint’s face. The Venetians came to Myra in 1100 and took what the men of Bari had left behind. The saint had left the East. He was about to belong to the whole Church.</p>

<h2 id="the-saint-who-comes-when-called">The Saint Who Comes When Called</h2>

<p>In Bari the tomb still gives its liquid, which the faithful call the manna of Saint Nicholas. The emperor Manuel, in one of his laws, mentioned a fragrant oily substance flowing from the saint at Bari. Each year on 9 May, the clergy draw it from the tomb. The bones were examined between 1953 and 1957 and laid in a new tomb in the crypt, and the manna, it is said, still flows.</p>

<p>Both Churches keep his feast on 6 December, the day of his death, and both keep 9 May as the feast of the translation of his relics to Bari. Saint Nikodemos of Mount Athos wrote a service for the translation, and in Greek lands the day falls on 9, 10, or 20 May. The Russian Church, which calls him Nikolai the Wonderworker, revived its feast of his birth, on 29 July, in 2004. In 1888 the Russian painter Repin went back to the oldest story of all and painted Nicholas saving the three innocent men at Myra.</p>

<p>In the Catholic West his patronages run to sailors, merchants, bakers, travelers, and children, and to cities and lands from Naples and Sicily to Liège and Freiburg. Russia and Moscow claim him too. And the gold through the window made him the saint of secret gifts, the distant ancestor of Santa Claus. It is a long way from Byrra. Yet even there the old pattern holds: a gift given in secret by someone who asked not to be thanked.</p>

<p>So who was he? Almost nothing about Nicholas of Myra can be proved, except that he was a bishop in the fourth century. But the Church’s memory of him is not a heap of loose tales. Across a thousand years, in Greek and Latin, Russian and Italian, it has kept a single face. He is the bishop running toward the sword, the young heir throwing gold through a window and asking for silence, and the man at the church door who called himself a sinner. He is the stranger in the emperor’s dream who would not let three innocent men die. And he is the plain cleric in the church at Myra whom the sailors finally knew as the one who had come to them in the storm.</p>

<p>That is why sailors and travelers, merchants and children, whole nations and single families still call on him. They call because of what he told the sailors, the words that every layer of his story repeats in its own way: “Behold, you have called me, and I am here to help you.”</p>

<h2 id="a-note-on-the-sources">A Note on the Sources</h2>

<p>The year of Nicholas’s death is given as 342, 343, 345, or 352, and no date is firm. Accounts of 1087 disagree in small ways. The first-night cures at Bari number forty-seven in Nicephorus and the Greek account, and thirty in Butler. The raiders the monks of Myra feared are Persians in one account and Turks in another. Butler dates the first church of Nicholas in Constantinople to about 430, while another source gives it to Justinian, who reigned from 527 to 565. The story of the dowry survives in three very old and very different versions. In the oldest there are two daughters and a son, and a young Nicholas takes the gold from his own living parents; another has four daughters. The pilgrimage to Jerusalem, the calming of the storm on that voyage, the title of abbot of Holy Sion, and probably the story of a tax relief won in Constantinople belong to Nicholas of Sion. Cioffari thinks the story of the sailors also fits the abbot better, though it stands in Michael’s Life of the bishop. The Venetian voyage of 1100 is known here only from later summaries.</p>

<h2 id="how-this-was-researched">How This Life Was Researched</h2>

<p>This life draws on twenty sources, read in English, with one in German. They include the <em>Praxis de stratelatis</em>, the oldest known account of Nicholas, and the ninth-century Life by Michael the Archimandrite. They also include the sermons of Proclus and Andrew of Crete, the <em>Golden Legend</em>, Butler’s <em>Lives of the Saints</em>, and two accounts of the translation to Bari, one written by Nicephorus of Bari in 1087. Every story was graded by how early and how independent its first witness is, from the <em>Praxis</em> to legends first written down a thousand years after his death. The original Greek and Latin texts were not read; every Greek and Latin source was read in an English translation or a scholar’s summary. The research flagged one detail, the size of the bribe in the story of the generals, that popular English retellings leave out.</p>

